ctice. More \nand more of our social goods are produced \nin hybrid marketplaces with mixed incen-\ntives for sharing ideas. Rather than simply \nassume an easy ﬁ t between idea sharing, \npeer networks, and nonproﬁ ts, I’d propose \nthinking of Future Perfect as an aspirational \nview of future opportunities.\nBy wandering far and wide from voting \nexperiments to municipal customer service \nlines, from crowdfunding platforms to em-\nployee-owned businesses, Johnson is seek-\ning common principles in a variety of \nspheres. What he ﬁ nds is a set of values that \nis internally consistent when seen against \nthe shared, open, and collectively mediated \nnature of the Internet. And, Johnson argues, \nit is inconsistent with the hierarchical, rep-\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAuthors’ Sources (https://hopewell-history.org/hvhist/Hopewell-History/Hw-Books-Historic/1982-Pennington-Straube-Center-Collectors-Edition-JJ.pdf)\nciteturn17search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; \nAuthors’ Sources\n\nPublished works:\n\nCunningham, John T. *This Is New Jersey.* (third edition). Rutgers University Press, 1978\n\nFisher, Douglas Alan. *The Epic of Steel.* Harper & Row, 1963.\n\nLewis, Alice Blackwell. *Hopewell Valley Heritage.* The Hopewell Museum, Hopewell, N.J., 1973.\n\nNeroni, Albert, *Mercer County Tercentenary.* Neroni Associates, Inc., 1964.\n\nO’Connell, Margaret J. *Pennington Profile: A Capsule of State and Nation,* 1966.\n\n“Pennington to Get Ambulance Through Gift by J.M. Cointreau.” Pennington-Lawrenceville supplement to *Princeton Herald,* April 4, 1941.\n\n“Liqueur Plant Badly Damaged by Flames.” *Trenton Times,* Jan. 4, 1967\n\nSweeney, Patrick J. “The Stills in Mercer’s Hills.” *Trenton Magazine,* October 1978.\n\nWeston, Loy. “The Entrepreneur Abroad.” *Far Eastern Economic Review,* Jan. 15-21, 1982.\n\nUnpublished sources:\n\nLewis, Alice Blackwell. Several typed pages